WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:06.780
&gt;&gt; Okay. So, to start off

00:00:06.781 --> 00:00:10.540
our last session we have
Eric Walkingshaw from OSU,

00:00:10.541 --> 00:00:12.650
who's going to talk

00:00:12.651 --> 00:00:16.415
to us about variational
programming. Please take it away.

00:00:16.416 --> 00:00:18.720
&gt;&gt; I'm up here from Oregon State.

00:00:18.721 --> 00:00:20.580
I'm one representative here.

00:00:20.581 --> 00:00:21.930
So, I wanted to briefly
show our group.

00:00:21.931 --> 00:00:24.300
So, this is just the
programming languages group,

00:00:24.301 --> 00:00:26.010
we're pretty small,
just two faculty members

00:00:26.011 --> 00:00:27.060
and some grad students,

00:00:27.061 --> 00:00:29.330
a couple grad students
were out sick on this day,

00:00:29.331 --> 00:00:31.530
unfortunately, but
and then we gathered

00:00:31.531 --> 00:00:34.860
about four software engineering
faculty members as well.

00:00:34.861 --> 00:00:36.690
So, yeah, I'm going

00:00:36.691 --> 00:00:37.990
to be talking about
variational programming,

00:00:37.991 --> 00:00:41.085
and this work was originally
motivated by a problem

00:00:41.086 --> 00:00:42.750
that comes up in the context of

00:00:42.751 --> 00:00:44.730
highly configurable
software systems.

00:00:44.731 --> 00:00:47.615
So, the idea here is that
you have a codebase,

00:00:47.616 --> 00:00:49.350
and this represents
not just one program,

00:00:49.351 --> 00:00:50.990
but lots of different programs.

00:00:50.991 --> 00:00:52.500
So, for example, it's

00:00:52.501 --> 00:00:54.750
a software product line or
it's an open source program

00:00:54.751 --> 00:00:56.310
that has lots of
configuration options

00:00:56.311 --> 00:00:58.555
and features that can
be turned on or off,

00:00:58.556 --> 00:01:01.380
and so the usual workflow for

00:01:01.381 --> 00:01:05.540
making a running program
out of this codebase,

00:01:05.541 --> 00:01:07.240
is the first thing that you
do is you configure it.

00:01:07.241 --> 00:01:08.860
So, you pick the features
that you want.

00:01:08.861 --> 00:01:11.580
I mean you generate a program
that can actually be

00:01:11.581 --> 00:01:13.280
compiled and then maybe you do

00:01:13.281 --> 00:01:15.190
some program analysis on that.

00:01:15.191 --> 00:01:16.235
Make sure that it works,

00:01:16.236 --> 00:01:19.285
run the test suite on it
and then if it works,

00:01:19.286 --> 00:01:21.050
you can go ahead and deploy it.

00:01:21.051 --> 00:01:23.290
So, the problem
with this workflow,

00:01:23.291 --> 00:01:26.295
especially in the context
of open source software,

00:01:26.296 --> 00:01:28.720
is that often the persons
that's generating

00:01:28.721 --> 00:01:32.540
and evaluating this program
that wants to deploy it,

00:01:32.541 --> 00:01:33.830
is not the same person that wrote

00:01:33.831 --> 00:01:35.470
the code in the
first place, right?

00:01:35.471 --> 00:01:37.170
So, the first person that
wrote the code they sort of

00:01:37.171 --> 00:01:39.755
understand all of
the configurations.

00:01:39.756 --> 00:01:41.870
The person that generates
a configuration,

00:01:41.871 --> 00:01:43.580
it's very likely that you can

00:01:43.581 --> 00:01:45.020
generate a configuration
that's never been

00:01:45.021 --> 00:01:47.210
generated before because
these things have

00:01:47.211 --> 00:01:49.830
hundreds or even thousands
of configuration options.

00:01:49.831 --> 00:01:51.020
They don't know what to do

00:01:51.021 --> 00:01:52.340
with the error that
they get back,

00:01:52.341 --> 00:01:54.795
they don't even maybe
maintain this code at all.

00:01:54.796 --> 00:01:57.270
So, this process
finds the errors too

00:01:57.271 --> 00:02:01.340
late but the person
that writes the code,

00:02:01.341 --> 00:02:03.380
they can't anticipate
all the configurations

00:02:03.381 --> 00:02:05.360
that somebody might
want to generate,

00:02:05.361 --> 00:02:07.365
there's just way
too many of them.

00:02:07.366 --> 00:02:10.090
So, the solution to this problem

00:02:10.091 --> 00:02:12.610
is something that we call
a variational analyses.

00:02:12.611 --> 00:02:14.165
So, what we do is,

00:02:14.166 --> 00:02:18.565
we instead evaluate
the configurable codebase

00:02:18.566 --> 00:02:21.165
directly and we call this
a variational analysis,

00:02:21.166 --> 00:02:22.420
and what this gives us,

00:02:22.421 --> 00:02:24.500
is it gives us
a variational result.

00:02:24.501 --> 00:02:25.930
The key thing here is that

00:02:25.931 --> 00:02:28.045
this variational result
conceptually

00:02:28.046 --> 00:02:30.790
represents the result for

00:02:30.791 --> 00:02:32.170
all of the configurations
that can be

00:02:32.171 --> 00:02:33.770
generated from this program.

00:02:33.771 --> 00:02:35.710
So, if we apply
a configuration to

00:02:35.711 --> 00:02:37.075
the original program and run

00:02:37.076 --> 00:02:38.650
the standard program analysis,

00:02:38.651 --> 00:02:40.690
we get the same
thing as if we apply

00:02:40.691 --> 00:02:43.415
that same configuration to
the variational result.

00:02:43.416 --> 00:02:46.500
So, now we can do things
like evaluate all

00:02:46.501 --> 00:02:48.990
of our configurations in advance

00:02:48.991 --> 00:02:50.490
so that the person that generates

00:02:50.491 --> 00:02:52.290
the configuration that they

00:02:52.291 --> 00:02:54.810
want to deploy doesn't
encounter an error.

00:02:54.811 --> 00:02:57.120
So, we've done lots of work on

00:02:57.121 --> 00:02:59.240
developing languages,
theory, data structures,

00:02:59.241 --> 00:03:02.100
and algorithms to do this sort
of thing correctly and

00:03:02.101 --> 00:03:04.290
efficiently and collectively we

00:03:04.291 --> 00:03:06.730
call this variational
programming.

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.390
So, the first example
of this that we did,

00:03:10.391 --> 00:03:12.115
so this is when I
was a grad student,

00:03:12.116 --> 00:03:13.920
is variational typing, so this is

00:03:13.921 --> 00:03:17.330
just sort of concrete instance
of this last slide here.

00:03:17.331 --> 00:03:19.529
So, in this context,

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:21.885
so normally you have
lambda calculus,

00:03:21.886 --> 00:03:23.265
you run the
Hindley-Milner algorithm,

00:03:23.266 --> 00:03:25.910
you get a type, you infer
a type for that program.

00:03:25.911 --> 00:03:28.924
So, here we have
a variational lambda calculus,

00:03:28.925 --> 00:03:31.160
and we can run
variational typing on

00:03:31.161 --> 00:03:33.660
that and get back
a variational type.

00:03:33.661 --> 00:03:36.980
The types that are
represented here correspond

00:03:36.981 --> 00:03:40.085
to all of the types of each
of the configurations.

00:03:40.086 --> 00:03:42.350
Okay. In order to implement

00:03:42.351 --> 00:03:44.810
this variational typing box here,

00:03:44.811 --> 00:03:46.780
we need variational programming.

00:03:46.781 --> 00:03:48.650
So, I'm going to give

00:03:48.651 --> 00:03:50.710
an idea of what variational
programming is by an example.

00:03:50.711 --> 00:03:53.405
This is sort of a fancy name
for a simple idea.

00:03:53.406 --> 00:03:55.600
So, the fundamental concept

00:03:55.601 --> 00:03:57.820
in variational programming is
something called a choice.

00:03:57.821 --> 00:04:00.425
So, here's an expression
with a choice in it.

00:04:00.426 --> 00:04:01.860
A choice has a dimension.

00:04:01.861 --> 00:04:04.990
So, in this case A
and two alternatives.

00:04:04.991 --> 00:04:06.610
So, we can select

00:04:06.611 --> 00:04:08.620
the left or the right
branch from a dimension,

00:04:08.621 --> 00:04:10.960
and we get the
corresponding alternative.

00:04:10.961 --> 00:04:12.340
So, if we were to

00:04:12.341 --> 00:04:14.660
evaluate this program
with a choice in it,

00:04:14.661 --> 00:04:16.615
we'd get back
a variational result

00:04:16.616 --> 00:04:19.305
with a choice in
the corresponding dimension.

00:04:19.306 --> 00:04:22.495
If we have multiple choices
in the same dimension,

00:04:22.496 --> 00:04:23.855
they're going to be synchronized.

00:04:23.856 --> 00:04:25.635
So, if we evaluate this,

00:04:25.636 --> 00:04:29.390
we'll get 12 and 23
because if we choose

00:04:29.391 --> 00:04:30.995
the left branch from A
we get the left branch

00:04:30.996 --> 00:04:33.415
of both choices in A.

00:04:33.416 --> 00:04:34.700
If we have choices in

00:04:34.701 --> 00:04:36.300
different dimensions,
they're independent.

00:04:36.301 --> 00:04:38.250
So, if we evaluate this program,

00:04:38.251 --> 00:04:40.100
we're going to get
four different alternatives,

00:04:40.101 --> 00:04:41.630
corresponding to
all the four different ways

00:04:41.631 --> 00:04:44.195
that we can configure
this expression.

00:04:44.196 --> 00:04:46.450
The alternatives of a choice can

00:04:46.451 --> 00:04:47.770
have different types as well.

00:04:47.771 --> 00:04:49.565
So, here's an example of a choice

00:04:49.566 --> 00:04:52.340
where one alternative
is a boolean and

00:04:52.341 --> 00:04:54.620
one alternative is
an integer and so

00:04:54.621 --> 00:04:57.250
we have choices also at
the type level here,

00:04:57.251 --> 00:05:00.170
so here a choice
in the expression

00:05:00.171 --> 00:05:04.970
induces a type with a choice
in the same dimension.

00:05:04.971 --> 00:05:07.315
So, here's another example

00:05:07.316 --> 00:05:11.660
of an expression with two
differently typed alternatives,

00:05:11.661 --> 00:05:13.780
and what we can see here

00:05:13.781 --> 00:05:16.110
is that when we have
a structured value,

00:05:16.111 --> 00:05:17.520
in this case, a type, we

00:05:17.521 --> 00:05:18.870
can push the choices
into the type.

00:05:18.871 --> 00:05:20.500
So, this type is
exactly equivalent

00:05:20.501 --> 00:05:22.870
to this choice type here,

00:05:22.871 --> 00:05:24.100
which more directly corresponds

00:05:24.101 --> 00:05:25.195
to the type of this expression.

00:05:25.196 --> 00:05:27.315
So, successor has
type Int to Int,

00:05:27.316 --> 00:05:29.710
even has type Int to
Bool and so we can

00:05:29.711 --> 00:05:32.800
factor this common
Int argument out.

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:37.929
Okay. So, of course,
we can evaluate.

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:44.210
So, here's another example
of applying an example,

00:05:44.211 --> 00:05:46.150
sorry applying two different
functions that have

00:05:46.151 --> 00:05:48.475
two different types to
two different values.

00:05:48.476 --> 00:05:50.895
So, the not function expects
a Boolean returns a Bool,

00:05:50.896 --> 00:05:52.810
even expects an Int
and returns a Bool.

00:05:52.811 --> 00:05:55.845
What we see here is
that in both cases,

00:05:55.846 --> 00:05:57.750
the result is false, right?

00:05:57.751 --> 00:05:59.380
So, we have two completely
different expressions

00:05:59.381 --> 00:06:01.415
not applied to true and
even applied to three,

00:06:01.416 --> 00:06:03.360
but both of them
returned false and

00:06:03.361 --> 00:06:05.170
so when we return here
is just the value false.

00:06:05.171 --> 00:06:06.725
So, we no longer have a choice.

00:06:06.726 --> 00:06:08.785
This is equivalent
to this expression,

00:06:08.786 --> 00:06:10.690
but we can forget
about the fact that we

00:06:10.691 --> 00:06:13.690
had a variation there because

00:06:13.691 --> 00:06:16.015
we don't need it going forward
and so this is going to be

00:06:16.016 --> 00:06:19.640
key to sort of computing
efficiently with these things.

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:22.780
Here's just a more
complicated example.

00:06:22.781 --> 00:06:25.230
Here this represents
four different expressions,

00:06:25.231 --> 00:06:26.330
successor applied to two,

00:06:26.331 --> 00:06:27.465
successor applied to four,

00:06:27.466 --> 00:06:29.040
and even applied to
both two and four.

00:06:29.041 --> 00:06:30.540
What we can see here is we get

00:06:30.541 --> 00:06:32.630
a more complicated
variational value,

00:06:32.631 --> 00:06:34.930
where one of the,

00:06:34.931 --> 00:06:37.860
where we had some sort
of folding inside of a

00:06:37.861 --> 00:06:41.445
or some sort of collapsing
inside of a choice.

00:06:41.446 --> 00:06:43.845
Okay. So, that's sort of just

00:06:43.846 --> 00:06:46.320
preserving variation
across computations.

00:06:46.321 --> 00:06:47.699
We can also reflect

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:49.270
on the variation that's
in a computation.

00:06:49.271 --> 00:06:51.480
So, here's an example
of a function vsum,

00:06:51.481 --> 00:06:53.190
I'm not showing how
this is implemented.

00:06:53.191 --> 00:06:54.360
But basically what this

00:06:54.361 --> 00:06:55.590
is going to do is this
is going to look at

00:06:55.591 --> 00:06:57.600
the alternatives that are in

00:06:57.601 --> 00:07:00.040
the results and sum them all up.

00:07:00.041 --> 00:07:02.200
So, it's going to sum up
all of the results here,

00:07:02.201 --> 00:07:06.110
so this is going to
sum 12 13 22 and

00:07:06.111 --> 00:07:07.980
23, which gives us 70.

00:07:07.981 --> 00:07:09.030
We don't have any variation

00:07:09.031 --> 00:07:09.990
in there anymore because we've

00:07:09.991 --> 00:07:12.465
accumulated all of
these alternatives.

00:07:12.466 --> 00:07:14.730
I mean we can also do
things like pick out

00:07:14.731 --> 00:07:17.000
a particular variation here.

00:07:17.001 --> 00:07:20.330
So here again we've got
the same expression,

00:07:20.331 --> 00:07:21.860
and what we want to know
is, we want to know

00:07:21.861 --> 00:07:23.960
what's the maximum
value in there.

00:07:23.961 --> 00:07:27.490
So, we get back the value
23, but oftentimes,

00:07:27.491 --> 00:07:30.335
we also want to know
which configuration

00:07:30.336 --> 00:07:32.240
that value came from and

00:07:32.241 --> 00:07:35.395
so we can return that
in the result as well.

00:07:35.396 --> 00:07:39.530
Okay. So, that's variational
programming by example.

00:07:39.531 --> 00:07:42.680
So, the first challenge
that comes in here is that

00:07:42.681 --> 00:07:45.395
the space of potential variants

00:07:45.396 --> 00:07:46.880
grows exponentially in the number

00:07:46.881 --> 00:07:48.360
of dimensions that you have.

00:07:48.361 --> 00:07:52.485
So, in the worst case this
is obviously very bad.

00:07:52.486 --> 00:07:54.500
So, the trick to sort of doing

00:07:54.501 --> 00:07:56.440
this is first of all to
share as much as possible.

00:07:56.441 --> 00:07:57.470
So, we don't want to introduce

00:07:57.471 --> 00:07:58.940
choices until we
absolutely have to,

00:07:58.941 --> 00:08:00.580
so that's what I mean
by splitting late.

00:08:00.581 --> 00:08:02.210
Anytime we have two alternatives

00:08:02.211 --> 00:08:03.740
that converge to the same value,

00:08:03.741 --> 00:08:06.395
we want to eliminate
choices as soon as we can.

00:08:06.396 --> 00:08:09.290
When we have structured data,

00:08:09.291 --> 00:08:10.880
we also need to use
clever data structures.

00:08:10.881 --> 00:08:11.780
I'll talk about that a little

00:08:11.781 --> 00:08:13.425
bit more here in just a second.

00:08:13.426 --> 00:08:15.410
But probably the
most important thing

00:08:15.411 --> 00:08:16.930
to do is just pick
the right domains.

00:08:16.931 --> 00:08:18.320
So, some domains don't have

00:08:18.321 --> 00:08:20.730
enough sharing in them
for this to really work.

00:08:20.731 --> 00:08:21.860
So, if you're doing numerical

00:08:21.861 --> 00:08:23.555
simulations or
something like that,

00:08:23.556 --> 00:08:25.685
this probably isn't
going to work.

00:08:25.686 --> 00:08:28.570
The other challenge is

00:08:28.571 --> 00:08:30.700
that variation is
sort of like sand.

00:08:30.701 --> 00:08:33.515
So, sand gets in everything,

00:08:33.516 --> 00:08:36.160
and variation also
gets in everything.

00:08:36.161 --> 00:08:36.900
So, when you're writing one

00:08:36.901 --> 00:08:38.605
of these variational analyses,

00:08:38.606 --> 00:08:40.830
the sand just gets in, the sand,

00:08:40.831 --> 00:08:41.970
the variation just gets into

00:08:41.971 --> 00:08:43.590
every incremental
data structure and

00:08:43.591 --> 00:08:46.030
every incremental value
that you're working with.

00:08:46.031 --> 00:08:49.325
So, in particular when
you have structured data,

00:08:49.326 --> 00:08:53.080
the idea of choices just sort
of starts to break down.

00:08:53.081 --> 00:08:54.930
So, here's a really
simple example where we

00:08:54.931 --> 00:08:57.305
have a choice between two lists.

00:08:57.306 --> 00:09:00.285
So, we have one, two, three
and one two, four, five.

00:09:00.286 --> 00:09:02.200
So, we can see that there
is some redundancy here.

00:09:02.201 --> 00:09:04.580
So, ideally we could factor
this out in some way.

00:09:04.581 --> 00:09:06.920
So, one idea is that
we sort of push

00:09:06.921 --> 00:09:09.600
the choices into
the list but then it's

00:09:09.601 --> 00:09:12.040
not really clear what to
do here because we have

00:09:12.041 --> 00:09:14.520
an element in one variant
and not in another.

00:09:14.521 --> 00:09:15.570
So, we need

00:09:15.571 --> 00:09:20.380
some specialized representation
for variational lists.

00:09:20.381 --> 00:09:22.230
It turns out we sort
of need lots of

00:09:22.231 --> 00:09:24.270
specialized variational
data structures

00:09:24.271 --> 00:09:28.925
for different kinds
of structured data.

00:09:28.926 --> 00:09:31.830
Originally, I had this vision
that this would all be very

00:09:31.831 --> 00:09:34.620
systematic and the variational

00:09:34.621 --> 00:09:36.315
data structures that worked best.

00:09:36.316 --> 00:09:38.205
So far seem to just be
ones that we've developed

00:09:38.206 --> 00:09:40.915
ad-hoc for each
particular domain.

00:09:40.916 --> 00:09:43.875
So, this is something that
really still needs more work.

00:09:43.876 --> 00:09:46.090
The other problem is what
to do about side effects.

00:09:46.091 --> 00:09:49.565
So, when we have choices in
programs and side effects,

00:09:49.566 --> 00:09:52.925
if you say print out
something inside of a choice,

00:09:52.926 --> 00:09:54.930
we can't make
two copies of the word,

00:09:54.931 --> 00:09:55.720
one where we printed out

00:09:55.721 --> 00:09:56.660
the value and when
were we didn't.

00:09:56.661 --> 00:09:57.910
So what do we do
there and this is

00:09:57.911 --> 00:10:00.800
something one of my grad
students is working on.

00:10:00.801 --> 00:10:03.520
Okay. So returning to
variational typing real quick.

00:10:03.521 --> 00:10:06.130
This is an ideal domain
for this problem.

00:10:06.131 --> 00:10:07.810
So, there's lots of sharing,

00:10:07.811 --> 00:10:09.910
lots of configurations
of a program end up

00:10:09.911 --> 00:10:12.450
having the same type,
so that's great.

00:10:12.451 --> 00:10:14.500
It turns out that for
realistic programs,

00:10:14.501 --> 00:10:17.430
the scales roughly linearly
with the size of the program.

00:10:17.431 --> 00:10:19.990
So, we don't encounter
this explosion of

00:10:19.991 --> 00:10:23.400
the variation space and

00:10:23.401 --> 00:10:26.410
this even scales to programs
as big as the Linux kernel.

00:10:26.411 --> 00:10:27.800
So, Christian Kastner at

00:10:27.801 --> 00:10:31.280
CMU implemented a tool
called type chef that is

00:10:31.281 --> 00:10:33.800
basically the same ideas
here and he can type

00:10:33.801 --> 00:10:36.930
check all the configurations
of the Linux kernel.

00:10:36.931 --> 00:10:40.755
It takes about a day
but it's doable.

00:10:40.756 --> 00:10:43.570
Okay. So, variational typing

00:10:43.571 --> 00:10:45.060
is sort of the ideal scenario.

00:10:45.061 --> 00:10:48.715
We've done this also for
several other- us and

00:10:48.716 --> 00:10:51.445
other people have done this
for several other analyses

00:10:51.446 --> 00:10:53.560
of software product lines,

00:10:53.561 --> 00:10:55.380
but it seems like this idea of

00:10:55.381 --> 00:10:57.040
choices should be more
general than that.

00:10:57.041 --> 00:10:58.580
So, for a while we

00:10:58.581 --> 00:11:00.640
sort of felt like we
had this cool hammer,

00:11:00.641 --> 00:11:04.680
and we're looking for
nails to hit with it.

00:11:04.681 --> 00:11:07.020
Which is always sort of
an awkward position to be in.

00:11:07.021 --> 00:11:09.155
It's not really the right
way to do research, right?

00:11:09.156 --> 00:11:13.045
But this is what we did I guess.

00:11:13.046 --> 00:11:15.330
So, we started thinking

00:11:15.331 --> 00:11:17.540
about what are the properties
that make a good nail.

00:11:17.541 --> 00:11:19.385
So, it needs to be something

00:11:19.386 --> 00:11:21.100
that has lots of variation in it

00:11:21.101 --> 00:11:23.180
because this is where this
variational programming idea

00:11:23.181 --> 00:11:25.000
really pays off.

00:11:25.001 --> 00:11:26.570
But it also needs to have lots

00:11:26.571 --> 00:11:28.465
of sharing between
the different variants.

00:11:28.466 --> 00:11:33.485
So, types good, numerical
simulations bad.

00:11:33.486 --> 00:11:36.500
Okay. So, sort of
the key insight was

00:11:36.501 --> 00:11:39.510
that the original problem or

00:11:39.511 --> 00:11:42.250
the original program that

00:11:42.251 --> 00:11:43.500
we're working with doesn't

00:11:43.501 --> 00:11:45.190
necessarily need to
have the variation,

00:11:45.191 --> 00:11:47.350
instead, we can use the idea of

00:11:47.351 --> 00:11:50.575
variation to explore
hypothetical situations.

00:11:50.576 --> 00:11:53.640
So, at points where

00:11:53.641 --> 00:11:56.635
sort of in a traditional,
say program analysis,

00:11:56.636 --> 00:11:57.930
we have to make
a decision to commit

00:11:57.931 --> 00:11:59.635
one path or the other,

00:11:59.636 --> 00:12:01.210
we can instead just introduce

00:12:01.211 --> 00:12:03.730
a choice and explore both paths.

00:12:03.731 --> 00:12:06.520
Okay, so let's look at
an example of that.

00:12:06.521 --> 00:12:12.795
Okay, so Michael talked
about fold localization.

00:12:12.796 --> 00:12:14.970
So, the problem of
error location in

00:12:14.971 --> 00:12:17.750
the context of type checking
is related to this.

00:12:17.751 --> 00:12:20.830
So, the idea here is that when
you run your type-checker,

00:12:20.831 --> 00:12:22.215
sometimes you get a type error,

00:12:22.216 --> 00:12:24.520
and locating where
the actual conceptual error

00:12:24.521 --> 00:12:26.390
that caused that type
error is a hard problem.

00:12:26.391 --> 00:12:28.085
It's been around since the 80s

00:12:28.086 --> 00:12:30.410
and I think the research
probably looks very similar

00:12:30.411 --> 00:12:32.185
to fold localization
where there's

00:12:32.186 --> 00:12:34.550
sort of all these new
approaches to this,

00:12:34.551 --> 00:12:35.970
and everyone's better
than the previous

00:12:35.971 --> 00:12:39.650
one according to
the papers, including ours.

00:12:41.300 --> 00:12:43.670
All right, so here's
an example of a program.

00:12:43.671 --> 00:12:44.910
So, there's no variation
in this program,

00:12:44.911 --> 00:12:46.840
this is just a plain
Haskell program

00:12:46.841 --> 00:12:48.745
that exhibits this problem.

00:12:48.746 --> 00:12:50.495
Okay, so here we've got,

00:12:50.496 --> 00:12:51.915
first at the top here we've got

00:12:51.916 --> 00:12:53.240
a definition of
the fold function,

00:12:53.241 --> 00:12:54.565
so this is a left fold.

00:12:54.566 --> 00:12:56.840
We've got flip which flips
the order of its arguments.

00:12:56.841 --> 00:12:59.575
We've got reverse implemented
in terms of fold and flip.

00:12:59.576 --> 00:13:02.059
Then finally we have
a function palindrome

00:13:02.060 --> 00:13:04.400
that checks to see if a list is

00:13:04.401 --> 00:13:06.540
a palindrome by
checking to see whether

00:13:06.541 --> 00:13:09.245
the original list is equal
to the reverse of the list.

00:13:09.246 --> 00:13:12.390
Does anybody see the bug
in this program?

00:13:15.640 --> 00:13:19.995
Okay. So, the bug
in this program,

00:13:19.996 --> 00:13:22.785
well let's see what GHC
has to say about it.

00:13:22.786 --> 00:13:24.675
So, here's what GHC says.

00:13:24.676 --> 00:13:28.390
So, GHC gives us
this sort of complicated,

00:13:28.391 --> 00:13:31.155
but ultimately it gives us
a location for the error.

00:13:31.156 --> 00:13:33.780
So, it says the problem is

00:13:33.781 --> 00:13:37.460
in reverse xs equals equals xs.

00:13:37.461 --> 00:13:40.495
Okay. That looks fine to me.

00:13:40.496 --> 00:13:42.450
So, it turns out
that's not really

00:13:42.451 --> 00:13:44.275
where the conceptual
source of the error.

00:13:44.276 --> 00:13:46.070
So, the conceptual source
of the error is that

00:13:46.071 --> 00:13:48.385
our base case of fold l is wrong.

00:13:48.386 --> 00:13:50.685
So, the very first line
of fold l is incorrect.

00:13:50.686 --> 00:13:51.870
That should just be,

00:13:51.871 --> 00:13:53.230
we should just return
the base case there,

00:13:53.231 --> 00:13:55.215
not a list containing
the base case.

00:13:55.216 --> 00:13:57.530
So, there is an error in
the base case of fold.

00:13:57.531 --> 00:13:58.910
But fold without

00:13:58.911 --> 00:14:01.505
any type annotations
actually type checks.

00:14:01.506 --> 00:14:03.995
The use of fold later

00:14:03.996 --> 00:14:06.405
in the reverse function
also type checks.

00:14:06.406 --> 00:14:11.080
We don't actually detect
the error until sort of this

00:14:11.081 --> 00:14:12.330
two uses later in

00:14:12.331 --> 00:14:14.110
the palindrome function when

00:14:14.111 --> 00:14:16.385
GHC finally detects the error.

00:14:16.386 --> 00:14:18.615
Okay, so this is the problem,

00:14:18.616 --> 00:14:22.525
and we can see it
exhibited here in GHC.

00:14:22.526 --> 00:14:25.140
Okay, so there's
been lots of work

00:14:25.141 --> 00:14:27.630
on how to improve error location.

00:14:27.631 --> 00:14:30.970
So, the solution, so
this has actually

00:14:30.971 --> 00:14:34.120
work by Senchen and Martin Erwig,

00:14:34.121 --> 00:14:35.950
so two of my collaborators,

00:14:35.951 --> 00:14:37.740
but this is a really
cool application,

00:14:37.741 --> 00:14:39.405
I think of variational
programming.

00:14:39.406 --> 00:14:41.330
So, basically the
problem is that locating

00:14:41.331 --> 00:14:43.560
the cause of a type
errors is hard.

00:14:43.561 --> 00:14:46.530
The reason is that type
inference commits too early.

00:14:46.531 --> 00:14:50.835
So, here when fold
type-checks correctly,

00:14:50.836 --> 00:14:54.020
it commits to the type
that it infers for fold.

00:14:54.850 --> 00:14:56.915
But the problem is that

00:14:56.916 --> 00:14:59.365
a successfully inferred type
could still be wrong.

00:14:59.366 --> 00:15:01.310
So, just because
type inference succeeded,

00:15:01.311 --> 00:15:02.640
doesn't necessarily
mean that type

00:15:02.641 --> 00:15:04.090
that was inferred
is the correct one.

00:15:04.091 --> 00:15:07.770
So, the solution is that
when we infer correct type,

00:15:07.771 --> 00:15:09.030
we still sort of keep open

00:15:09.031 --> 00:15:10.735
the possibility that
that type could be wrong.

00:15:10.736 --> 00:15:12.610
So, we assume that

00:15:12.611 --> 00:15:15.690
the type either could be correct
or could still be wrong.

00:15:15.691 --> 00:15:19.160
So, the way counterfactual
typing works

00:15:19.161 --> 00:15:22.490
is that we basically perform
variational type inference.

00:15:22.491 --> 00:15:25.130
So, error tolerant
here means that if

00:15:25.131 --> 00:15:29.240
one particular branch of
type inference fails,

00:15:29.241 --> 00:15:31.995
we don't fail the whole process.

00:15:31.996 --> 00:15:33.890
This is important because
we're dealing with

00:15:33.891 --> 00:15:36.110
a program of type errors in it?

00:15:36.111 --> 00:15:37.560
But for every subexpression,

00:15:37.561 --> 00:15:39.030
so every single subexpression in

00:15:39.031 --> 00:15:41.550
our program when we

00:15:41.551 --> 00:15:44.825
infer a type T for
the subexpression e,

00:15:44.826 --> 00:15:46.950
we instead introduced
a choice type here.

00:15:46.951 --> 00:15:49.910
So, we instead say e is

00:15:49.911 --> 00:15:51.050
a choice and sum dimension

00:15:51.051 --> 00:15:53.325
d that's either the type
that we inferred,

00:15:53.326 --> 00:15:54.825
or it's a fresh
type variable here.

00:15:54.826 --> 00:15:56.520
So, the dimension is fresh and

00:15:56.521 --> 00:16:00.070
the type variable is fresh.
We do this everywhere.

00:16:00.071 --> 00:16:02.960
When we're done, we get
this variational result

00:16:02.961 --> 00:16:05.540
with lots of dimensions
in it and we're going to

00:16:05.541 --> 00:16:07.740
search that variational
type that we get

00:16:07.741 --> 00:16:11.500
back for a value that's
not an error type,

00:16:11.501 --> 00:16:15.400
and that has as few
right selections as possible.

00:16:15.401 --> 00:16:18.180
So, right selections
correspond to places

00:16:18.181 --> 00:16:21.230
where the inferred
type was wrong.

00:16:21.231 --> 00:16:23.940
So, we want a type
where we have to,

00:16:23.941 --> 00:16:26.180
this basically is a measure
of how many changes we have

00:16:26.181 --> 00:16:28.685
to make in order to
fix our program.

00:16:28.686 --> 00:16:29.720
So, it's basically
saying what was

00:16:29.721 --> 00:16:31.585
written here was wrong,

00:16:31.586 --> 00:16:34.240
and so we're sort of using

00:16:34.241 --> 00:16:35.490
this type variable to capture

00:16:35.491 --> 00:16:37.405
that we might have put
anything else in there,

00:16:37.406 --> 00:16:39.285
anything with a different type.

00:16:39.286 --> 00:16:40.820
So, we search
our variational type

00:16:40.821 --> 00:16:41.630
for something where we have

00:16:41.631 --> 00:16:44.090
to make as few right
choices as possible.

00:16:44.091 --> 00:16:46.200
It turns out that this

00:16:46.201 --> 00:16:48.710
gives us the location
where we have

00:16:48.711 --> 00:16:53.180
to make the smallest number
of changes to find the error,

00:16:53.181 --> 00:16:55.860
or to fix the error as possible.

00:16:55.861 --> 00:16:58.180
Then we apply
some other heuristics that

00:16:58.181 --> 00:17:01.270
help us be better than
everybody else before us.

00:17:01.271 --> 00:17:03.775
Okay, so that's one problem.

00:17:03.776 --> 00:17:05.860
Oh shoot, I'm slightly over.

00:17:05.861 --> 00:17:08.510
So, we also apply this
to gradual typing.

00:17:08.511 --> 00:17:09.790
It's really cool, but I took way

00:17:09.791 --> 00:17:11.795
too long explaining
the other thing.

00:17:11.796 --> 00:17:14.335
So, let me just say real quick,

00:17:14.336 --> 00:17:16.050
so gradual typing is all

00:17:16.051 --> 00:17:18.295
about mixing static
and dynamic types,

00:17:18.296 --> 00:17:20.480
but a big problem with them is

00:17:20.481 --> 00:17:23.375
migrating between
graduate type programs,

00:17:23.376 --> 00:17:26.500
so the problems
that can occur are

00:17:26.501 --> 00:17:29.590
that when you're adding
type annotations,

00:17:29.591 --> 00:17:31.645
you can get into a place

00:17:31.646 --> 00:17:34.680
where you sort of can't add
anymore type annotations,

00:17:34.681 --> 00:17:37.035
but you're not in the most
statically type program.

00:17:37.036 --> 00:17:39.600
There's also potential for
huge performance degradation.

00:17:39.601 --> 00:17:42.730
So, there's papers written
about how gradual typing

00:17:42.731 --> 00:17:44.190
is broken fundamentally
because of

00:17:44.191 --> 00:17:46.280
this performance
degradation problem.

00:17:46.281 --> 00:17:47.890
So, basically the idea
is that we do

00:17:47.891 --> 00:17:50.520
the same trick that we did
in counterfactual typing,

00:17:50.521 --> 00:17:52.480
and we can basically

00:17:52.481 --> 00:17:54.820
introduce choice types
for every parameter,

00:17:54.821 --> 00:17:56.420
and explore the whole space of

00:17:56.421 --> 00:17:58.370
potential gradual
type integrations,

00:17:58.371 --> 00:17:59.710
and find ones that satisfy

00:17:59.711 --> 00:18:01.805
different criteria that we want.

00:18:01.806 --> 00:18:04.460
So, this paper was
at POPL this year.

00:18:04.461 --> 00:18:06.330
I put little crossed fingers next

00:18:06.331 --> 00:18:08.080
to ICFP. Our reviews were good.

00:18:08.081 --> 00:18:09.810
So, hopefully in
a week we'll find

00:18:09.811 --> 00:18:11.930
out if we have
an ICFP paper on it too.

00:18:11.931 --> 00:18:13.815
Okay, so sorry I went over

00:18:13.816 --> 00:18:16.800
variational programming
makes variation explicit,

00:18:16.801 --> 00:18:18.330
and we can use this to explore

00:18:18.331 --> 00:18:21.770
hypothetical scenarios. Thanks.

00:18:27.480 --> 00:18:30.010
Okay. Are there any questions for

00:18:30.011 --> 00:18:32.930
Eric, about variational
programming?

00:18:34.250 --> 00:18:37.720
It's late in the
afternoon, don't be shy.

00:18:38.140 --> 00:18:41.160
One thing I was curious about.

00:18:41.350 --> 00:18:44.710
You mentioned like
simulations as a bad option,

00:18:44.711 --> 00:18:45.860
but actually there's a lot of

00:18:45.861 --> 00:18:47.300
simulation packages that have

00:18:47.301 --> 00:18:50.675
a lot of variations for accuracy.

00:18:50.676 --> 00:18:52.910
So, like Seagull, is the one
that sticks out in my mind,

00:18:52.911 --> 00:18:54.860
where they had like
80 different flavors

00:18:54.861 --> 00:18:56.020
depending on how
much are you willing

00:18:56.021 --> 00:18:57.775
to spend on floating
point. I wonder-

00:18:57.776 --> 00:18:59.960
&gt;&gt; So, I guess the reason I

00:18:59.961 --> 00:19:02.020
assume numerical simulations
will be bad is because

00:19:02.021 --> 00:19:03.700
we never have this situation

00:19:03.701 --> 00:19:07.695
where alternatives converge
to the same value.

00:19:07.696 --> 00:19:09.760
So, in typing, this happens a lot

00:19:09.761 --> 00:19:11.330
because different
configurations have

00:19:11.331 --> 00:19:12.645
the same type and so we can

00:19:12.646 --> 00:19:15.015
forget about
the choices basically,

00:19:15.016 --> 00:19:19.410
but yeah, I don't know, we
can talk about this more.

00:19:19.411 --> 00:19:20.430
Maybe in this case where it's

00:19:20.431 --> 00:19:22.825
different amounts of precision,
maybe this does occur.

00:19:22.826 --> 00:19:24.660
&gt;&gt; Well actually the problem
is they don't converge back,

00:19:24.661 --> 00:19:25.815
so you're totally right there.

00:19:25.816 --> 00:19:27.530
That's why there's
BIF. All right.

00:19:27.531 --> 00:19:29.195
Any other questions for Eric?

00:19:29.196 --> 00:19:31.335
Okay. So, up next.

00:19:31.336 --> 00:19:33.965
Oh, I'm so sorry, Doug.

00:19:33.966 --> 00:19:36.530
&gt;&gt; I have no idea if this is
a good question, but, so,

00:19:36.531 --> 00:19:40.610
you mentioned that you've
had trouble finding anything

00:19:40.611 --> 00:19:42.575
general with variational
data structures?

00:19:42.576 --> 00:19:44.330
Is there something
about derivatives

00:19:44.331 --> 00:19:46.400
of types that would help here?

00:19:46.401 --> 00:19:48.080
It seems like your variational

00:19:48.081 --> 00:19:49.150
data structures are
like you're you're

00:19:49.151 --> 00:19:50.545
modifying something in some way

00:19:50.546 --> 00:19:52.225
you want to record
those modifications?

00:19:52.226 --> 00:19:54.250
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, I thought about

00:19:54.251 --> 00:19:56.820
this like using zippers
or something like this.

00:19:56.821 --> 00:19:59.420
Yeah. So, I still think there's
potential for this here,

00:19:59.421 --> 00:20:04.760
but problem is just
that you can get

00:20:04.761 --> 00:20:07.455
better performance than
introducing choices

00:20:07.456 --> 00:20:09.250
in a derivative of a data type

00:20:09.251 --> 00:20:11.420
by just doing something special.

00:20:11.421 --> 00:20:14.200
So, I mean, this is hard
to say without examples,

00:20:14.201 --> 00:20:17.350
but I'm here, we can talk
about this offline maybe.

00:20:17.351 --> 00:20:21.270
&gt;&gt; Awesome. Okay. Let's
thank Eric again.

00:20:24.520 --> 00:20:27.460
So up next, Brad is going to
tell us about what's been

00:20:27.461 --> 00:20:30.020
going on with the Chapel
Language at Cray.

00:20:30.021 --> 00:20:32.555
&gt;&gt; Great. Thanks. So,
I'm Brad Chamberlain,

00:20:32.556 --> 00:20:33.950
I'm a Principal Engineer at Cray,

00:20:33.951 --> 00:20:35.740
here in Seattle, and I'm
the technical lead for

00:20:35.741 --> 00:20:37.215
the Chapel Project which I'll be

00:20:37.216 --> 00:20:38.905
talking a bit about today.

00:20:38.906 --> 00:20:43.475
This is the official
title for my talk,

00:20:43.476 --> 00:20:45.175
but I have this bad
habit of making up

00:20:45.176 --> 00:20:47.395
dumb titles the night
before I give talks,

00:20:47.396 --> 00:20:50.020
so this'll make more sense
as we go along.

00:20:50.021 --> 00:20:52.075
If you're not
familiar with Chapel,

00:20:52.076 --> 00:20:53.450
it's a productive parallel

00:20:53.451 --> 00:20:55.360
programming language that
we're developing at Cray.

00:20:55.361 --> 00:20:56.755
It's portable and scalable,

00:20:56.756 --> 00:20:58.945
which means it can run on
your laptop, your cluster,

00:20:58.946 --> 00:21:00.620
the cloud on a Cray,

00:21:00.621 --> 00:21:02.250
and it's open source
and collaborative,

00:21:02.251 --> 00:21:03.970
which means we're
developing it in GitHub,

00:21:03.971 --> 00:21:06.290
under the Apache license.

00:21:06.291 --> 00:21:08.220
The two main goals of Chapel are

00:21:08.221 --> 00:21:09.680
to support general
parallel programming,

00:21:09.681 --> 00:21:10.920
which you can think of as,

00:21:10.921 --> 00:21:12.930
if you've got a parallel
algorithm in your mind and

00:21:12.931 --> 00:21:14.960
some parallel hardware
you'd like to run it on,

00:21:14.961 --> 00:21:16.580
Chapel should be able to do that.

00:21:16.581 --> 00:21:18.860
The second goal is to
make parallel programming

00:21:18.861 --> 00:21:21.140
at scale far more productive
than it is today.

00:21:21.141 --> 00:21:22.590
Now, that term productive

00:21:22.591 --> 00:21:24.330
means different things
to different people.

00:21:24.331 --> 00:21:25.820
One way that I'll explain it

00:21:25.821 --> 00:21:27.820
is to compare to other languages.

00:21:27.821 --> 00:21:30.840
So, one language that's easy
to read and write code in,

00:21:30.841 --> 00:21:32.010
let's say a Python,

00:21:32.011 --> 00:21:34.310
but yet is as fast as a Fortran,

00:21:34.311 --> 00:21:35.530
as scalable as MPI,

00:21:35.531 --> 00:21:36.690
which is how most people do

00:21:36.691 --> 00:21:40.535
large-scale HPC programming
today, as portable as C,

00:21:40.536 --> 00:21:42.345
as flexible as C++

00:21:42.346 --> 00:21:44.260
and as fun as your favorite
programming language,

00:21:44.261 --> 00:21:46.795
so that you get excited
about using Chapel.

00:21:46.796 --> 00:21:49.740
So, one way that we'll
visualize how we're

00:21:49.741 --> 00:21:50.880
doing here is by looking at

00:21:50.881 --> 00:21:52.690
the Computer Language
Benchmarks Game,

00:21:52.691 --> 00:21:54.720
and here I've taken the
languages that are involved

00:21:54.721 --> 00:21:56.760
in the game and I've plotted
two points for each one,

00:21:56.761 --> 00:21:59.400
which are the geometric means
of their fastest programs

00:21:59.401 --> 00:22:02.070
and their smallest programs
in terms of code size.

00:22:02.071 --> 00:22:04.410
So, the closer they are to
the bottom of the slide,

00:22:04.411 --> 00:22:05.740
the faster the languages.

00:22:05.741 --> 00:22:06.990
The closer they are to the left,

00:22:06.991 --> 00:22:09.295
the smaller the codes
are in the language.

00:22:09.296 --> 00:22:11.670
So as you'd expect,
the scripting languages are

00:22:11.671 --> 00:22:14.530
further off to the left
here but much slower,

00:22:14.531 --> 00:22:15.970
and if we zoom in on who's down

00:22:15.971 --> 00:22:17.630
there at the right, we see C,

00:22:17.631 --> 00:22:19.415
C++, Fortran and rest,

00:22:19.416 --> 00:22:21.710
but of course their codes
are also much bigger.

00:22:21.711 --> 00:22:23.200
Now, I've left Chapel off here,

00:22:23.201 --> 00:22:24.720
but if we add the Chapel
entries to this,

00:22:24.721 --> 00:22:27.035
then were off here
down to the left,

00:22:27.036 --> 00:22:28.700
which you can think of as
being productive because

00:22:28.701 --> 00:22:32.450
you're fast and small,

00:22:32.451 --> 00:22:34.600
and if I zoom back
out, we're again,

00:22:34.601 --> 00:22:36.410
uniquely down to
the left compared to

00:22:36.411 --> 00:22:38.615
where most other
languages fit nowadays.

00:22:38.616 --> 00:22:43.170
Now of course, we all know
that language succinctness

00:22:43.171 --> 00:22:45.775
isn't necessarily
the best metric of productivity.

00:22:45.776 --> 00:22:47.460
So, the website also lets you

00:22:47.461 --> 00:22:49.310
go in and look at the programs,
and for the Chapel,

00:22:49.311 --> 00:22:51.880
you can see things like
these high-level cobegin

00:22:51.881 --> 00:22:54.455
again and coforall constructs,
which create tasks,

00:22:54.456 --> 00:22:55.680
whereas in the C entry, you can

00:22:55.681 --> 00:22:57.400
see things like sched_getaffinity

00:22:57.401 --> 00:22:58.890
and things that hopefully most of

00:22:58.891 --> 00:23:01.170
you have never had to
deal with in your life.

00:23:01.171 --> 00:23:03.880
So, that's a little bit
about Chapel.

00:23:03.881 --> 00:23:06.070
When I was approaching
this talk, I said, well,

00:23:06.071 --> 00:23:07.880
I could talk about
a number of things and

00:23:07.881 --> 00:23:08.970
my reviewers each wanted me to

00:23:08.971 --> 00:23:10.515
talk about something different,

00:23:10.516 --> 00:23:12.365
which I should have anticipated.

00:23:12.366 --> 00:23:13.805
But one of the quotes
that stuck out

00:23:13.806 --> 00:23:15.200
for me was this one which said,

00:23:15.201 --> 00:23:17.170
"Chapel has been around
for quite awhile,

00:23:17.171 --> 00:23:18.900
and it still seems like
a niche language."

00:23:18.901 --> 00:23:21.425
Which is one of the things
that hurts your heart here,

00:23:21.426 --> 00:23:23.865
but is arguably true

00:23:23.866 --> 00:23:25.750
and I think a lot of people
share this perspective.

00:23:25.751 --> 00:23:28.535
So, I wanted to dive into
this a little bit and say,

00:23:28.536 --> 00:23:30.940
first of all, are we
a niche language?

00:23:30.941 --> 00:23:32.900
One way in which you
could view us as being

00:23:32.901 --> 00:23:34.800
niche is that Chapel
was originally

00:23:34.801 --> 00:23:37.180
designed for HPC and HPC

00:23:37.181 --> 00:23:40.115
is arguably a niche field
within computer science.

00:23:40.116 --> 00:23:42.760
But that said, if we step
back and look at Chapel,

00:23:42.761 --> 00:23:45.050
Chapel cares about performance
and I think a lot of

00:23:45.051 --> 00:23:47.510
people who even outside of
HPC care about performance,

00:23:47.511 --> 00:23:49.420
we've heard a lot
about that today.

00:23:49.421 --> 00:23:51.940
Chapel cares about
programmability and I think

00:23:51.941 --> 00:23:53.130
the rise of things like Python

00:23:53.131 --> 00:23:54.720
show that people care about that.

00:23:54.721 --> 00:23:56.480
Chapel cares about
parallelism and

00:23:56.481 --> 00:23:58.245
multicore means that
we're all arguably

00:23:58.246 --> 00:24:00.610
able to be parallel
programmers nowadays and it

00:24:00.611 --> 00:24:03.665
also cares about scalability
or distributed memory,

00:24:03.666 --> 00:24:05.110
and with the rise
of cloud computing,

00:24:05.111 --> 00:24:06.130
you can see that that has

00:24:06.131 --> 00:24:07.995
become bigger and
bigger over time.

00:24:07.996 --> 00:24:10.155
So, I would argue that Chapel,

00:24:10.156 --> 00:24:12.285
while developed
originally for HPC,

00:24:12.286 --> 00:24:13.715
actually has a lot
of applicability

00:24:13.716 --> 00:24:15.405
outside of HPC as well,

00:24:15.406 --> 00:24:17.480
and as the language matures
we're hearing that from a lot

00:24:17.481 --> 00:24:20.520
of non HPC programmers as well.

00:24:20.521 --> 00:24:23.710
Another way you could consider
us niche is that we really

00:24:23.711 --> 00:24:26.545
have a very modest sized
user community,

00:24:26.546 --> 00:24:28.860
like it's not a Python,

00:24:28.861 --> 00:24:30.665
we'd love to be as

00:24:30.666 --> 00:24:33.005
large and successful
community as Python is.

00:24:33.006 --> 00:24:35.795
But something you have to
know is that historically,

00:24:35.796 --> 00:24:37.140
if you came to me and said,

00:24:37.141 --> 00:24:39.590
"Should I program my next
production app in Chapel?"

00:24:39.591 --> 00:24:41.040
Historically, my answer has been,

00:24:41.041 --> 00:24:42.760
no probably not yet,

00:24:42.761 --> 00:24:44.885
and that gets me to
the quiche part of the talk.

00:24:44.886 --> 00:24:47.300
So, I think a lot of people
in the community who

00:24:47.301 --> 00:24:49.890
maybe heard about Chapel
over its lifetime say like,

00:24:49.891 --> 00:24:52.845
"Why aren't more people using
this delectable language?"

00:24:52.846 --> 00:24:54.630
In their mind they've
got this picture

00:24:54.631 --> 00:24:56.850
of this lovely quiche
that we've been making.

00:24:56.851 --> 00:24:58.410
But the reality is that over

00:24:58.411 --> 00:25:01.725
the large fraction of
the program's history,

00:25:01.726 --> 00:25:03.320
we've been putting
this quiche together

00:25:03.321 --> 00:25:06.080
and you probably don't
want to eat it just yet,

00:25:06.081 --> 00:25:07.700
but read the recipe,

00:25:07.701 --> 00:25:09.400
sample the ingredients,
see if you think we're

00:25:09.401 --> 00:25:11.480
on a path that's going
to be attractive to you.

00:25:11.481 --> 00:25:13.470
I say where we are today is here

00:25:13.471 --> 00:25:16.065
where it's perhaps
almost fully baked,

00:25:16.066 --> 00:25:17.825
and maybe it's time to
take a look at it again,

00:25:17.826 --> 00:25:19.780
if it's something that's
of interest to you.

00:25:19.781 --> 00:25:22.080
So, going to the other part
of that reviewer comment

00:25:22.081 --> 00:25:23.610
that has been around
for quite a while,

00:25:23.611 --> 00:25:25.075
so that's accurate as well.

00:25:25.076 --> 00:25:27.290
Chapel came out of
the DARPA HPCS program

00:25:27.291 --> 00:25:29.450
that ran from 2003 to 2012.

00:25:29.451 --> 00:25:32.065
At that time, we were
very much research focus,

00:25:32.066 --> 00:25:34.005
a small team of 6-7 people,

00:25:34.006 --> 00:25:35.290
and I think we accomplished

00:25:35.291 --> 00:25:38.160
some great research objectives

00:25:38.161 --> 00:25:40.130
in the parallel language area,

00:25:40.131 --> 00:25:42.140
but at the end of the day we
had a research prototype,

00:25:42.141 --> 00:25:44.185
we didn't have something
that people can really use.

00:25:44.186 --> 00:25:45.800
So, what we've been
doing since then is

00:25:45.801 --> 00:25:47.545
what we call the five-year push.

00:25:47.546 --> 00:25:49.190
This has been
a five-year effort to

00:25:49.191 --> 00:25:52.910
basically move Chapel from
research more to product,

00:25:52.911 --> 00:25:55.370
and we doubled the size of
the team as part of that,

00:25:55.371 --> 00:25:57.040
and we focused on
these three main areas:

00:25:57.041 --> 00:25:58.700
performance and scalability,

00:25:58.701 --> 00:26:00.040
building up the ecosystem around

00:26:00.041 --> 00:26:01.730
Chapel and fixing things in

00:26:01.731 --> 00:26:03.230
the base language
that weren't directly

00:26:03.231 --> 00:26:05.755
related to parallelism
or scalability.

00:26:05.756 --> 00:26:07.430
This third one is probably of

00:26:07.431 --> 00:26:09.045
most interest to this crowd,

00:26:09.046 --> 00:26:11.260
but doesn't fit at all
in a 10 minute talk.

00:26:11.261 --> 00:26:13.775
So, I'm going to touch
slightly on the first two,

00:26:13.776 --> 00:26:15.455
and I'm going to compare then,

00:26:15.456 --> 00:26:17.030
where were we at the end of HPCS

00:26:17.031 --> 00:26:18.170
and the start of this push,

00:26:18.171 --> 00:26:20.150
versus now, where are we today?

00:26:20.151 --> 00:26:23.435
I'll start with an ecosystem
then versus now.

00:26:23.436 --> 00:26:25.295
So, when we came out of HPCS,

00:26:25.296 --> 00:26:26.530
in terms of documentation,

00:26:26.531 --> 00:26:28.500
we had a PDF language
specification,

00:26:28.501 --> 00:26:30.070
we had a Quick Reference sheet,

00:26:30.071 --> 00:26:33.040
we had a number of text-based
README files and we had

00:26:33.041 --> 00:26:34.420
some primer examples
that taught you

00:26:34.421 --> 00:26:36.445
the language through
comments and code.

00:26:36.446 --> 00:26:38.470
Now, we're at a point
where we have over

00:26:38.471 --> 00:26:41.505
200 modern hyperlinked web
based documentation pages,

00:26:41.506 --> 00:26:43.340
and when you surf to
Chapel, it feels more like

00:26:43.341 --> 00:26:45.860
a real language and less
like somebody's experiment.

00:26:45.861 --> 00:26:48.260
In terms of libraries
at the end of HPCS,

00:26:48.261 --> 00:26:50.205
we had about 25 library modules,

00:26:50.206 --> 00:26:52.250
some of them were
documented in source code,

00:26:52.251 --> 00:26:54.290
some of them were not
really documented at all.

00:26:54.291 --> 00:26:55.470
We're now at a point
where we have

00:26:55.471 --> 00:26:57.420
about 60 library modules,

00:26:57.421 --> 00:26:59.400
all of them are
document on the web

00:26:59.401 --> 00:27:01.855
and many of these have
been user contributed.

00:27:01.856 --> 00:27:05.480
Then in terms of tools,
at the end of HPCS,

00:27:05.481 --> 00:27:07.650
we had highlighting
modes for emacs and vim,

00:27:07.651 --> 00:27:09.635
we had chpldoc or
at least a rough draft,

00:27:09.636 --> 00:27:12.280
which was a documentation
tool and now we

00:27:12.281 --> 00:27:15.490
have those as well as
mason package manager,

00:27:15.491 --> 00:27:17.840
c2chapel aid for
interoperability,

00:27:17.841 --> 00:27:20.780
chpltags which allows you
to search your source code,

00:27:20.781 --> 00:27:22.780
bash tab completion and then this

00:27:22.781 --> 00:27:25.559
performance visualizer over
on the side here chplvis,

00:27:25.560 --> 00:27:28.955
so a lot of great improvements
to the ecosystem.

00:27:28.956 --> 00:27:30.740
Performance is
another thing which has

00:27:30.741 --> 00:27:32.690
traditionally been a
problem for Chapel.

00:27:32.691 --> 00:27:35.095
If you ask most people in
HPC about Chapel they say,

00:27:35.096 --> 00:27:36.310
"Oh, it's that really
pretty language

00:27:36.311 --> 00:27:37.890
that performs really terribly."

00:27:37.891 --> 00:27:41.055
In HPC, of course,
that's a killer.

00:27:41.056 --> 00:27:43.050
So, over the last five years

00:27:43.051 --> 00:27:44.390
we've done a lot
with performance,

00:27:44.391 --> 00:27:46.130
this is a bit of
a laundry list, but in short,

00:27:46.131 --> 00:27:48.000
we've done a lot of
locality oriented things,

00:27:48.001 --> 00:27:50.100
particularly within
multi-socket nodes

00:27:50.101 --> 00:27:52.885
and a lot of communication
reduction across nodes.

00:27:52.886 --> 00:27:55.530
So, if I went back
to where we then,

00:27:55.531 --> 00:27:56.920
this is a stream performance

00:27:56.921 --> 00:27:58.440
benchmark and higher is better,

00:27:58.441 --> 00:28:01.545
and I'm adding nodes across
the axis of the bottom here.

00:28:01.546 --> 00:28:03.810
So, you could see that
as we added nodes,

00:28:03.811 --> 00:28:05.825
the performance got
better as you'd expect,

00:28:05.826 --> 00:28:07.955
but if you compare that
to where we are today,

00:28:07.956 --> 00:28:10.170
we've already have a third

00:28:10.171 --> 00:28:11.635
of the performance
we're getting today,

00:28:11.636 --> 00:28:13.320
and of course now you should
be suspicious and say,

00:28:13.321 --> 00:28:15.010
well, how do we know
that you're good today?

00:28:15.011 --> 00:28:15.840
Well, if we compare to

00:28:15.841 --> 00:28:17.120
the reference version
in green here,

00:28:17.121 --> 00:28:18.910
you can see that we're
tracking it pretty closely,

00:28:18.911 --> 00:28:21.510
we're just a little bit of
hit due to our elegance.

00:28:21.511 --> 00:28:23.270
Looking in second example,

00:28:23.271 --> 00:28:25.060
this is a Stencil computation at

00:28:25.061 --> 00:28:27.210
that end of HPCS time period.

00:28:27.211 --> 00:28:29.840
Again, it's getting
faster as we add nodes,

00:28:29.841 --> 00:28:31.475
but if we add today's onto their,

00:28:31.476 --> 00:28:33.760
basically the red line goes
all the way down to the axis,

00:28:33.761 --> 00:28:37.120
we just were terrible in terms
of raw performance then,

00:28:37.121 --> 00:28:38.650
and again if I had been
the reference you can

00:28:38.651 --> 00:28:40.975
see that we're tracking
it very closely today.

00:28:40.976 --> 00:28:43.195
So, you can look at this across

00:28:43.196 --> 00:28:45.355
a number of HPC computations.

00:28:45.356 --> 00:28:47.080
These handle things like

00:28:47.081 --> 00:28:49.680
just Local loops,
Embarrassing Parallelism,

00:28:49.681 --> 00:28:53.050
Bucket-Exchange, Stencils,
Global Random Updates,

00:28:53.051 --> 00:28:54.320
and in all these cases were doing

00:28:54.321 --> 00:28:56.455
quite well compared to
the reference version.

00:28:56.456 --> 00:28:58.300
I'll go into one more,
in a bit more detail,

00:28:58.301 --> 00:29:00.260
the Global Random Updates here.

00:29:00.261 --> 00:29:03.385
This is the code for the MPI
version of this benchmark,

00:29:03.386 --> 00:29:06.525
as you can see, it's almost
as elegant as Chapel.

00:29:06.526 --> 00:29:09.390
At the top, there is a comment
and this comment tells

00:29:09.391 --> 00:29:12.215
you what this code does.

00:29:12.216 --> 00:29:13.580
Basically, it's just doing

00:29:13.581 --> 00:29:15.970
random updates to globally
distributed table.

00:29:15.971 --> 00:29:17.755
This is the chapel loop for

00:29:17.756 --> 00:29:19.550
that same kernel
and as you can see

00:29:19.551 --> 00:29:21.020
the actual computation is about

00:29:21.021 --> 00:29:23.650
the size of the of
the MPI computation.

00:29:23.651 --> 00:29:25.075
This is another case
where we're not only

00:29:25.076 --> 00:29:26.870
scaling competitively with
the reference version,

00:29:26.871 --> 00:29:28.535
but in fact outperforming it.

00:29:28.536 --> 00:29:30.585
Okay, so what's next for us?

00:29:30.586 --> 00:29:32.890
Next week, we've got
our annual workshop.

00:29:32.891 --> 00:29:33.930
It'll be up in Vancouver.

00:29:33.931 --> 00:29:35.630
So it's localish this year.

00:29:35.631 --> 00:29:37.930
If you've got friends or
colleagues or students who are

00:29:37.931 --> 00:29:38.975
interested in parallel
computing and

00:29:38.976 --> 00:29:40.540
languages, send them up.

00:29:40.541 --> 00:29:43.370
It's generally considered
a high-energy workshop,

00:29:43.371 --> 00:29:44.865
and as we look forward,

00:29:44.866 --> 00:29:47.080
we're kicking off
a new three-year effort

00:29:47.081 --> 00:29:48.300
where we're going
to continue to work

00:29:48.301 --> 00:29:49.790
on performance and scalability.

00:29:49.791 --> 00:29:52.015
I'm targeting Libfabric,
targeting GPUs,

00:29:52.016 --> 00:29:55.100
targeting the cloud
and working on AI.

00:29:55.101 --> 00:29:56.970
This is our team at Cray.

00:29:56.971 --> 00:29:58.325
Again, they're about 13 of us

00:29:58.326 --> 00:30:00.010
working on it full-time
at any given time.

00:30:00.011 --> 00:30:01.265
We also have
an intern program and

00:30:01.266 --> 00:30:03.185
we'd love to have
your students apply to that.

00:30:03.186 --> 00:30:04.785
It's an open source efforts.

00:30:04.786 --> 00:30:06.460
We have community
partners as well.

00:30:06.461 --> 00:30:08.715
You notice there's a sort
of disturbing lack

00:30:08.716 --> 00:30:11.130
of civic Northwest education and

00:30:11.131 --> 00:30:13.370
industry on here and we'd
love to correct that if

00:30:13.371 --> 00:30:16.270
anyone would like to
collaborate in this area.

00:30:16.271 --> 00:30:19.180
I think just to leave you here,

00:30:19.181 --> 00:30:22.115
I will just skip ahead
to my summary slide.

00:30:22.116 --> 00:30:25.300
So we've made huge progress
over the past five years.

00:30:25.301 --> 00:30:27.605
We think chapels
ready for production,

00:30:27.606 --> 00:30:28.995
but I put an asterisk
there because

00:30:28.996 --> 00:30:30.320
different people have
different ideas what

00:30:30.321 --> 00:30:33.375
production means and again
we're open to collaborations.

00:30:33.376 --> 00:30:34.960
Last thing I'll mention
is that we have

00:30:34.961 --> 00:30:37.210
some chapel swag in
the form of screen or

00:30:37.211 --> 00:30:39.170
glasses wipes up here on
the chair if you need

00:30:39.171 --> 00:30:42.290
one and I've got
five minutes for questions.

00:30:50.500 --> 00:30:52.675
&gt;&gt; I can start off.
I have a question.

00:30:52.676 --> 00:30:53.045
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:30:53.046 --> 00:30:56.455
&gt;&gt; So you showed how
versus at the end

00:30:56.456 --> 00:31:00.050
of the Darpa Program, HPCS.

00:31:00.051 --> 00:31:00.755
&gt;&gt; HPCS.

00:31:00.756 --> 00:31:02.595
&gt;&gt; Yeah, the end of HPCS,

00:31:02.596 --> 00:31:05.060
compared to that, your
performance is way, way better.

00:31:05.061 --> 00:31:05.470
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:31:05.471 --> 00:31:06.930
&gt;&gt; Is the code the same?

00:31:06.931 --> 00:31:08.830
Are you running the same program

00:31:08.831 --> 00:31:09.890
and getting that much
better performance

00:31:09.891 --> 00:31:12.910
or are the implementations
updated as well?

00:31:12.911 --> 00:31:14.270
&gt;&gt; Yeah. No, so in this case,

00:31:14.271 --> 00:31:16.975
what we did was we
took today's code

00:31:16.976 --> 00:31:19.005
and we actually took
that old release

00:31:19.006 --> 00:31:20.210
in random space hardware.

00:31:20.211 --> 00:31:22.025
So it's as apples to
apples as possible.

00:31:22.026 --> 00:31:23.950
In some cases, we have
to take the code and

00:31:23.951 --> 00:31:26.290
maybe sometimes we relied
on a newer feature.

00:31:26.291 --> 00:31:28.215
We had to rewrite that using
a slightly older feature

00:31:28.216 --> 00:31:29.745
in order to get it to
run on the old version,

00:31:29.746 --> 00:31:32.650
but by and large, it's the

00:31:32.651 --> 00:31:34.985
more or less the same user code
that you would write.

00:31:34.986 --> 00:31:36.845
So, yeah. So it's meant to be

00:31:36.846 --> 00:31:38.520
as fair like comparison
as possible,

00:31:38.521 --> 00:31:40.545
and it just speaks to how

00:31:40.546 --> 00:31:43.485
poor we were in that time frame
in terms of performance.

00:31:43.486 --> 00:31:44.910
&gt;&gt; Another thing
I'm curious about.

00:31:44.911 --> 00:31:46.670
So in this distributed setting,

00:31:46.671 --> 00:31:48.160
I mean if you have some kind

00:31:48.161 --> 00:31:50.225
of work stealing or something,

00:31:50.226 --> 00:31:52.280
depending on how the schedule
ends up you might

00:31:52.281 --> 00:31:54.180
do floating point operations
in a different order.

00:31:54.181 --> 00:31:54.485
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:31:54.486 --> 00:31:56.075
&gt;&gt; Do you ever worry about that

00:31:56.076 --> 00:31:59.015
like even just reproducibility?

00:31:59.016 --> 00:32:00.720
&gt;&gt; So, right. So, certainly,

00:32:00.721 --> 00:32:02.875
reproducibility is a concern

00:32:02.876 --> 00:32:04.820
among some of Cray's customers

00:32:04.821 --> 00:32:06.900
and they sort of
want things just so.

00:32:06.901 --> 00:32:08.340
We don't actually do
much in the way of

00:32:08.341 --> 00:32:10.215
work stealing at
least across nodes,

00:32:10.216 --> 00:32:11.850
but still it's a parallel
language you tend to

00:32:11.851 --> 00:32:13.765
have operations happening in

00:32:13.766 --> 00:32:14.915
different orders and
things like that.

00:32:14.916 --> 00:32:17.190
So we have ways of kind

00:32:17.191 --> 00:32:19.590
of locking down order,
but, of course,

00:32:19.591 --> 00:32:21.270
at the expense of parallelism and

00:32:21.271 --> 00:32:23.440
therefore maybe performance
for people who

00:32:23.441 --> 00:32:25.695
do need that reproducibility.

00:32:25.696 --> 00:32:27.835
But in general, you can render
the sorts of errors like

00:32:27.836 --> 00:32:30.185
we saw in the floating
point talk earlier.

00:32:30.186 --> 00:32:31.590
If you do things in
the wrong order,

00:32:31.591 --> 00:32:33.325
like you may get
wildly different results

00:32:33.326 --> 00:32:34.450
and that sort of goes along with

00:32:34.451 --> 00:32:36.005
parallel computing
and unfortunately as

00:32:36.006 --> 00:32:38.195
the world we're used to
living in for the most part.

00:32:38.196 --> 00:32:40.000
&gt;&gt; Do you guys have sort of like

00:32:40.001 --> 00:32:41.625
regression tests to ensure that,

00:32:41.626 --> 00:32:43.140
I guess you have
some flag that's like

00:32:43.141 --> 00:32:44.425
make this reproducible.

00:32:44.426 --> 00:32:44.905
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:32:44.906 --> 00:32:46.340
&gt;&gt; It seems they get
the whole way down, right?

00:32:46.341 --> 00:32:47.400
There's both on the network,

00:32:47.401 --> 00:32:49.050
there's across cores and
then even within the core.

00:32:49.051 --> 00:32:50.990
If you want to exploit
70 a lot of times,

00:32:50.991 --> 00:32:52.640
you'll do some enrolling
in reassociation.

00:32:52.641 --> 00:32:53.920
So I mean there's

00:32:53.921 --> 00:32:54.920
so many levels for

00:32:54.921 --> 00:32:56.860
reproducibility for
these kinds of large jobs.

00:32:56.861 --> 00:32:58.000
How do you make sure that you've

00:32:58.001 --> 00:33:00.525
actually found all of them?
That seems really tough.

00:33:00.526 --> 00:33:02.220
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I don't think we
have a really good solution

00:33:02.221 --> 00:33:03.470
there to be perfectly honest.

00:33:03.471 --> 00:33:04.930
&gt;&gt; It's interesting,
research challenge.

00:33:04.931 --> 00:33:05.980
If somebody in the region is

00:33:05.981 --> 00:33:07.525
looking to collaborate,
maybe you could think about.

00:33:07.526 --> 00:33:10.190
&gt;&gt; We would love
collaborations around,

00:33:10.191 --> 00:33:12.390
and I think you guys have
the chops for it as well.

00:33:12.391 --> 00:33:15.485
I see two hands here, Charlie.

00:33:15.486 --> 00:33:17.855
&gt;&gt; So, you said
you're cloud-ready.

00:33:17.856 --> 00:33:20.410
How easy is a pre-startup say

00:33:20.411 --> 00:33:22.705
100 chapel nodes on
someone's cloud?

00:33:22.706 --> 00:33:25.055
&gt;&gt; So, today,

00:33:25.056 --> 00:33:27.580
the main cloud that we've had
the most experience with is

00:33:27.581 --> 00:33:30.660
AWS and there's sort of
a Read Me that tells you how

00:33:30.661 --> 00:33:32.140
to grab the thing and set up

00:33:32.141 --> 00:33:33.820
your machine image
just so and build it.

00:33:33.821 --> 00:33:35.245
We've talked about
building a sort of

00:33:35.246 --> 00:33:36.790
pre-deployed machine
image so you can

00:33:36.791 --> 00:33:37.520
just grab it without

00:33:37.521 --> 00:33:38.590
having to do the build
and everything,

00:33:38.591 --> 00:33:40.210
but we've never quite
cross that path.

00:33:40.211 --> 00:33:41.570
We're interesting in
doing the same for

00:33:41.571 --> 00:33:43.105
the other major cloud providers.

00:33:43.106 --> 00:33:44.610
So if any of you
happen to work for

00:33:44.611 --> 00:33:46.455
them and would be
influencing that,

00:33:46.456 --> 00:33:48.075
we'd love to work
with you on that.

00:33:48.076 --> 00:33:51.670
Yeah. But our implementation
is very portable.

00:33:51.671 --> 00:33:54.340
So, generally speaking,
most of the cloud providers

00:33:54.341 --> 00:33:55.660
have some sort of Linux kind of

00:33:55.661 --> 00:33:57.770
option and that tends
to work fine for us.

00:33:57.771 --> 00:33:59.995
Okay. Question at
the back there maybe.

00:33:59.996 --> 00:34:02.305
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So if

00:34:02.306 --> 00:34:04.505
you want to run
these algorithms on the cloud,

00:34:04.506 --> 00:34:08.270
then it's probably longer
running algorithms.

00:34:08.380 --> 00:34:12.010
Some machines don't
work as intended.

00:34:12.011 --> 00:34:13.835
What do you do about [inaudible]?

00:34:13.836 --> 00:34:17.440
&gt;&gt; Yes, so I would
say that we share,

00:34:17.441 --> 00:34:19.610
the HPCS has traditionally have

00:34:19.611 --> 00:34:22.155
this theme of like just
run fast, and if it fails,

00:34:22.156 --> 00:34:24.480
run again or checkpoint restart,

00:34:24.481 --> 00:34:26.265
and we haven't done anything

00:34:26.266 --> 00:34:28.500
in chapel so far any
better than that.

00:34:28.501 --> 00:34:30.665
So if the machine fails,

00:34:30.666 --> 00:34:34.660
we don't sort of do anything
magical or automatic there,

00:34:34.661 --> 00:34:37.130
and that's another area
where we'd like to do

00:34:37.131 --> 00:34:39.405
additional research potentially
in a collaborative manner.

00:34:39.406 --> 00:34:40.300
So, again, if that's

00:34:40.301 --> 00:34:41.915
a topic of interest on
people in the room,

00:34:41.916 --> 00:34:44.600
I would be very interested
on talking me about that.

00:34:45.240 --> 00:34:46.805
&gt;&gt; Cool.

00:34:46.806 --> 00:34:47.205
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:34:47.206 --> 00:34:49.120
&gt;&gt; Let's thank Brad again.

00:34:54.860 --> 00:34:57.060
&gt;&gt; Okay. So, up next,

00:34:57.061 --> 00:34:58.710
we have John Leo.

00:34:58.711 --> 00:35:02.665
He's going to tell us about
type theory and music.

00:35:02.666 --> 00:35:05.370
That's something you
hear combined every day.

00:35:05.371 --> 00:35:07.920
It's ornaments in music.

00:35:07.921 --> 00:35:12.610
If I know John, there's going
to be types in there. Okay.

00:35:12.611 --> 00:35:13.065
&gt;&gt; For sure.

00:35:13.066 --> 00:35:19.260
&gt;&gt; All right, so I would
like to start out with

00:35:19.261 --> 00:35:22.440
some a little bit of
background and motivation

00:35:22.441 --> 00:35:24.100
and then I'll talk
a little bit about kind

00:35:24.101 --> 00:35:26.330
of a fun project I've
been working on.

00:35:26.331 --> 00:35:28.295
This is available online,

00:35:28.296 --> 00:35:31.120
but it's still very preliminary.

00:35:31.121 --> 00:35:34.045
So it will probably
change quite drastically.

00:35:34.046 --> 00:35:36.640
So I'd like to start out
with my favorite quote by

00:35:36.641 --> 00:35:39.285
Bob Harper which is worth
savoring for a moment.

00:35:39.286 --> 00:35:41.760
"Eventually all the arbitrary
programming languages

00:35:41.761 --> 00:35:43.400
are going to just be swept
away with the oceans,

00:35:43.401 --> 00:35:45.110
and we will have the permanence
of constructive,

00:35:45.111 --> 00:35:46.525
intuitionistic type theory as

00:35:46.526 --> 00:35:47.980
the master theory of computation,

00:35:47.981 --> 00:35:49.940
without doubt in
my mind, no question.

00:35:49.941 --> 00:35:51.300
So, from my point of view,

00:35:51.301 --> 00:35:52.430
this is a personal statement,

00:35:52.431 --> 00:35:55.005
working in anything else
is a waste of time."

00:35:55.006 --> 00:35:58.500
So I love this quote.
I agree with that.

00:35:58.501 --> 00:36:00.945
I'm really into both math and

00:36:00.946 --> 00:36:04.870
computer science and I love
to see this convergence.

00:36:04.871 --> 00:36:07.105
So the second question asks,

00:36:07.106 --> 00:36:09.960
what will programming
look like in 50 years?

00:36:09.961 --> 00:36:14.015
I kind of agree with
Bob's vision about this.

00:36:14.016 --> 00:36:16.210
We already have kind of
things pointing the way.

00:36:16.211 --> 00:36:17.295
There's functional programming.

00:36:17.296 --> 00:36:18.630
There's a lot of
work been done in

00:36:18.631 --> 00:36:22.970
algebra programming
mostly based at Oxford,

00:36:23.090 --> 00:36:27.910
actually, using math
in your programming.

00:36:27.911 --> 00:36:30.135
Then, now, that
was mostly done in

00:36:30.136 --> 00:36:32.835
the context of
pure functional programming,

00:36:32.836 --> 00:36:34.340
but now we have dependent types
where you can do

00:36:34.341 --> 00:36:35.860
even more math and

00:36:35.861 --> 00:36:37.445
then there's even newer
things coming out.

00:36:37.446 --> 00:36:41.020
Now, there's cubicle type
theory and so forth.

00:36:41.021 --> 00:36:43.110
But there's also a question, who

00:36:43.111 --> 00:36:44.740
does the programming because,

00:36:44.741 --> 00:36:47.155
I mean, certainly,
from my experiences,

00:36:47.156 --> 00:36:48.670
most programmers hate math.

00:36:48.671 --> 00:36:51.470
So they're really willing
to learn all this.

00:36:51.471 --> 00:36:53.380
But I don't know, maybe
50 years from now.

00:36:53.381 --> 00:36:55.705
The computers will be
programming themselves.

00:36:55.706 --> 00:36:57.310
But I think they'll be doing it,

00:36:57.311 --> 00:36:59.775
written there and
programming and type theory.

00:36:59.776 --> 00:37:01.445
All right, so given,

00:37:01.446 --> 00:37:03.960
this is the goal, how do
we actually get there?

00:37:03.961 --> 00:37:08.330
So we have some finally some kind

00:37:08.331 --> 00:37:10.050
of industrial strength of

00:37:10.051 --> 00:37:12.995
functional programming languages
Haskell and OCaml.

00:37:12.996 --> 00:37:15.215
So we can try adding
independent types to

00:37:15.216 --> 00:37:17.260
them and certainly that's
being done for Haskell,

00:37:17.261 --> 00:37:19.525
and even now in Haskell
you can kind of fake

00:37:19.526 --> 00:37:20.835
dependent types using a ton of

00:37:20.836 --> 00:37:22.950
extensions, but it's very ugly.

00:37:22.951 --> 00:37:25.340
Another way to do
it is just takes

00:37:25.341 --> 00:37:27.890
some language that was designed

00:37:27.891 --> 00:37:29.985
from the start for dependent
type something like add

00:37:29.986 --> 00:37:32.350
their interests and try to
make it practical to use.

00:37:32.351 --> 00:37:33.870
I think it'll be
interesting to see

00:37:33.871 --> 00:37:36.320
which one of those two wins out.

00:37:36.321 --> 00:37:39.760
Meanwhile, how do we even
program using dependent types?

00:37:39.761 --> 00:37:42.550
This is really something
that no one's done yet.

00:37:42.551 --> 00:37:43.745
I mean, no one's trying to write

00:37:43.746 --> 00:37:46.270
dependently type programming
for industry or whatever.

00:37:46.271 --> 00:37:48.280
I've done functional programming

00:37:48.281 --> 00:37:49.980
in industry and even then it's

00:37:49.981 --> 00:37:52.805
not exactly clear what's

00:37:52.806 --> 00:37:54.595
the right way to write
functional programming stuff.

00:37:54.596 --> 00:37:56.425
It's still kind of
even in infancy,

00:37:56.426 --> 00:38:00.575
independent types is even
not even up to that level.

00:38:00.576 --> 00:38:02.780
It's clear there's still
going to be a lot of

00:38:02.781 --> 00:38:04.640
both theoretical and
practical advances

00:38:04.641 --> 00:38:06.120
needed to get to this point.

00:38:06.121 --> 00:38:08.135
Say with these are all
interesting things to look at,

00:38:08.136 --> 00:38:09.940
what I want to
concentrate here on

00:38:09.941 --> 00:38:13.030
is let's actually start

00:38:13.031 --> 00:38:15.870
trying to program
using dependent types.

00:38:16.620 --> 00:38:19.125
So going back a little bit.

00:38:19.126 --> 00:38:21.720
When I was first learning
Haskell in 2014,

00:38:21.721 --> 00:38:24.090
I came across
this wonderful book by

00:38:24.091 --> 00:38:26.570
Paul Hudak and a system

00:38:26.571 --> 00:38:28.140
that he had written
called Euterpea,

00:38:28.141 --> 00:38:30.635
which was a system to do music,

00:38:30.636 --> 00:38:32.990
and I'm very interested
in music and I've done

00:38:32.991 --> 00:38:35.440
a little composition
and stuff like that.

00:38:35.441 --> 00:38:37.950
But I'm very lazy because

00:38:37.951 --> 00:38:40.180
I don't want to have to
spend a lot of time on it.

00:38:40.181 --> 00:38:41.525
I actually have
some music software,

00:38:41.526 --> 00:38:45.320
but none of it's been quite
what I wanted to use.

00:38:45.321 --> 00:38:47.200
But the Euterpea was

00:38:47.201 --> 00:38:48.470
actually close to what
I don't want to do.

00:38:48.471 --> 00:38:50.090
Here is something where
we could represent music.

00:38:50.091 --> 00:38:51.405
Using a programming language,

00:38:51.406 --> 00:38:53.685
you could kind of do
mathy types of things with it.

00:38:53.686 --> 00:38:56.755
So it seemed very cool to use.

00:38:56.756 --> 00:39:01.350
So, but the utopia

00:39:01.351 --> 00:39:03.480
was kind of based on old Haskell
and stuff like that.

00:39:03.481 --> 00:39:04.560
I thought well maybe I can make

00:39:04.561 --> 00:39:07.100
a better more modern version.

00:39:07.101 --> 00:39:11.510
So, I started working on
something called music tools.

00:39:11.511 --> 00:39:13.165
It's just basically,
the idea is as

00:39:13.166 --> 00:39:16.600
small toolkit of
very small programs

00:39:16.601 --> 00:39:17.930
that have a very

00:39:17.931 --> 00:39:21.010
specific purpose and you can
combine them together using

00:39:21.011 --> 00:39:22.920
functional programming
techniques or whatever,

00:39:22.921 --> 00:39:24.670
and with the idea

00:39:24.671 --> 00:39:28.170
to help you synthesize and
analyze music and so forth.

00:39:28.171 --> 00:39:30.010
So, I originally
wrote it in Haskell,

00:39:30.011 --> 00:39:32.020
but then I thought going
back to this thing

00:39:32.021 --> 00:39:34.030
of dependent types are
the greatest thing,

00:39:34.031 --> 00:39:36.230
why am I not using them.

00:39:36.231 --> 00:39:37.590
So, I tried converting it to

00:39:37.591 --> 00:39:40.470
AGDA and actually
it's interesting

00:39:40.471 --> 00:39:42.250
because actually even
found some bugs in

00:39:42.251 --> 00:39:44.720
the Haskell version when I
made the types more specific.

00:39:44.721 --> 00:39:47.145
So, it actually already was
paying off a little bit.

00:39:47.146 --> 00:39:49.765
I saw these Haskell
would actually

00:39:49.766 --> 00:39:53.285
get anything to have
IO and so forth.

00:39:53.286 --> 00:39:55.270
But it actually worked
out pretty well

00:39:55.271 --> 00:39:57.950
and I think it's a good domain

00:39:57.951 --> 00:39:59.905
to actually start with because

00:39:59.906 --> 00:40:03.060
dependent type languages are
not exactly performing yet,

00:40:03.061 --> 00:40:04.150
but here's something
where we don't

00:40:04.151 --> 00:40:06.565
need incredible performance.

00:40:06.566 --> 00:40:08.305
We can kind of do
stuff in batch mode

00:40:08.306 --> 00:40:11.335
and it's still reasonably fast.

00:40:11.336 --> 00:40:14.450
And it's a nice domain, right?

00:40:14.451 --> 00:40:16.960
Music is fairly
well-described and so forth,

00:40:16.961 --> 00:40:18.825
but it's still fairly rich.

00:40:18.826 --> 00:40:22.230
And then the exciting
thing too is,

00:40:22.231 --> 00:40:23.880
I've been doing some research

00:40:23.881 --> 00:40:26.130
with Talia and Nate and Dan,

00:40:26.131 --> 00:40:27.370
and some of the stuff that we're

00:40:27.371 --> 00:40:29.520
doing look like it would
apply to this as well.

00:40:29.521 --> 00:40:33.030
So, I thought well, here's
a chance I can play with this.

00:40:34.630 --> 00:40:39.140
So, I wasn't sure if I'd
have time to play any music,

00:40:39.141 --> 00:40:40.400
but I have some requests,

00:40:40.401 --> 00:40:42.360
so maybe I'll see if this works.

00:40:42.361 --> 00:40:44.340
So, here's a little piece
that I wrote,

00:40:44.341 --> 00:40:46.860
a very simple song
I wrote back in

00:40:46.861 --> 00:40:49.580
the day and this is

00:40:49.581 --> 00:40:53.780
actually I kind of
re-implemented it in.

00:40:55.200 --> 00:41:01.545
We'll see if this
works, well, maybe not.

00:41:01.546 --> 00:41:03.185
All right. Well,
sorry about that.

00:41:03.186 --> 00:41:04.390
&gt;&gt; You might just
try to play it on

00:41:04.391 --> 00:41:07.285
your speakers on the laptop.

00:41:07.286 --> 00:41:08.880
&gt;&gt; Yeah, but then I
have to figure out how

00:41:08.881 --> 00:41:10.155
to change the audio output,

00:41:10.156 --> 00:41:12.870
so anyway, don't worry about it.

00:41:12.871 --> 00:41:16.810
But, I show it to you
a little bit what it looks like.

00:41:16.811 --> 00:41:19.380
So basically,

00:41:20.290 --> 00:41:23.620
there's kind of four lines
here a melody, accompaniment,

00:41:23.621 --> 00:41:26.335
bass and drums and

00:41:26.336 --> 00:41:30.850
the melody I essentially
just wrote out for now,

00:41:30.851 --> 00:41:32.620
but there's actually
some structure to it

00:41:32.621 --> 00:41:34.850
and I could take
advantage of that or

00:41:34.851 --> 00:41:36.540
maybe everyone what's
maybe interested in is how

00:41:36.541 --> 00:41:39.650
the program can figure out
the structure and recreate it.

00:41:39.651 --> 00:41:41.530
But as you can see for

00:41:41.531 --> 00:41:43.290
the accompaniment has
a little more structure,

00:41:43.291 --> 00:41:44.320
so we're actually able to use

00:41:44.321 --> 00:41:45.440
some functional
programming things,

00:41:45.441 --> 00:41:48.155
you see maps and zips and
stuff like that in there.

00:41:48.156 --> 00:41:51.840
And the bass and the drums
even more structures,

00:41:51.841 --> 00:41:53.490
you can make them
even more minimal.

00:41:53.491 --> 00:41:54.670
In fact, the very first thing I

00:41:54.671 --> 00:41:55.980
did with utopia was tried to

00:41:55.981 --> 00:41:58.370
write minimum version
of the hands on

00:41:58.371 --> 00:42:04.160
exercises with them if you
know those piano exercises.

00:42:04.161 --> 00:42:06.920
All right. So anyway, to

00:42:06.921 --> 00:42:08.320
get this to work and
have a nice tool,

00:42:08.321 --> 00:42:10.390
we need a good
representation of music,

00:42:10.391 --> 00:42:11.610
how do you represent music?

00:42:11.611 --> 00:42:13.860
And I've thought
about it for a while,

00:42:13.861 --> 00:42:16.380
and I don't think is
a perfect representation.

00:42:16.381 --> 00:42:18.110
There's a lot of good ways

00:42:18.111 --> 00:42:20.155
to do it and they all
have pluses and minuses.

00:42:20.156 --> 00:42:21.950
So, right now I'm using something

00:42:21.951 --> 00:42:23.915
that's pretty close
to utopia where

00:42:23.916 --> 00:42:25.640
pitches and durations
they kind of

00:42:25.641 --> 00:42:27.280
make notes out of them
and then music out of

00:42:27.281 --> 00:42:29.520
that and you can compose things

00:42:29.780 --> 00:42:32.305
sequentially or in parallel.

00:42:32.306 --> 00:42:37.520
But, you may notice there's
some ambiguity here,

00:42:37.521 --> 00:42:39.310
you can kind of represent music

00:42:39.311 --> 00:42:41.630
and in multiple ways
that sound equivalent.

00:42:41.631 --> 00:42:43.180
There's equivalence
kind of structurally,

00:42:43.181 --> 00:42:45.395
there's equivalent
musical equivalence

00:42:45.396 --> 00:42:47.590
and this is something utopia
has to deal with as well.

00:42:47.591 --> 00:42:49.780
For example, you can have
zero length rests in there,

00:42:49.781 --> 00:42:52.580
which actually is
a common thing you'll do if

00:42:52.581 --> 00:42:56.300
you're folding and near
zero length is your base case.

00:42:56.301 --> 00:42:59.255
So, here's just

00:42:59.256 --> 00:43:02.480
even a more concrete example
for pitch itself,

00:43:02.481 --> 00:43:03.570
we can represent as a sort of

00:43:03.571 --> 00:43:05.450
absolute pitch which
I have at the top,

00:43:05.451 --> 00:43:06.710
but then we could
also represent it

00:43:06.711 --> 00:43:08.090
as a relative pitch within

00:43:08.091 --> 00:43:11.650
an octave and then
paired with the octave.

00:43:11.651 --> 00:43:14.750
So now, if we wrote all these
functions using one,

00:43:14.751 --> 00:43:16.030
we want to actually
use them using

00:43:16.031 --> 00:43:17.215
the other one, what do you do?

00:43:17.216 --> 00:43:19.670
You can convert back
and forth and so forth.

00:43:19.671 --> 00:43:21.705
But, the nice thing is there's

00:43:21.706 --> 00:43:23.570
some nice research being

00:43:23.571 --> 00:43:25.450
done on this that we can
actually apply here.

00:43:25.451 --> 00:43:29.470
So, the homotopy type theory
gives you a way

00:43:29.471 --> 00:43:33.550
to first define equivalence

00:43:33.551 --> 00:43:35.310
and then lift it up to functions

00:43:35.311 --> 00:43:36.815
based on that equivalence.

00:43:36.816 --> 00:43:38.840
And now, since we're
using independent types,

00:43:38.841 --> 00:43:40.720
we can take advantage of

00:43:40.721 --> 00:43:43.110
this research and actually do it.

00:43:43.111 --> 00:43:44.960
So, there's been
some work on this,

00:43:44.961 --> 00:43:46.950
there's a paper equivalences
for free which might

00:43:46.951 --> 00:43:49.450
also appear on ICFP we'll see,

00:43:49.451 --> 00:43:52.360
done by some people
in Inria, in Caulk,

00:43:52.361 --> 00:43:54.920
and I've been moving this

00:43:54.921 --> 00:43:58.135
over to the AGDA world
and using it for that.

00:43:58.136 --> 00:43:59.510
But there's still some problems

00:43:59.511 --> 00:44:00.390
here even though you do get

00:44:00.391 --> 00:44:02.840
a lot of equivalences for free,

00:44:02.841 --> 00:44:04.740
just the base equivalences

00:44:04.741 --> 00:44:09.500
themselves can be rather
painful to define,

00:44:09.501 --> 00:44:11.590
and it seems like this

00:44:11.591 --> 00:44:13.470
could be automated
in some way and I

00:44:13.471 --> 00:44:15.520
think the work actually
Talia is doing

00:44:15.521 --> 00:44:18.555
now should be very
very helpful for this.

00:44:18.556 --> 00:44:21.710
So, even beyond equivalences,

00:44:21.711 --> 00:44:23.330
so my definition of

00:44:23.331 --> 00:44:25.520
music I left out something
that was in who'd axe,

00:44:25.521 --> 00:44:28.445
you had an extra little thing,
this modify control.

00:44:28.446 --> 00:44:31.150
And so,

00:44:31.151 --> 00:44:33.845
this actually has some extra
information and in fact,

00:44:33.846 --> 00:44:37.030
you can even put
ornaments in your music.

00:44:37.560 --> 00:44:41.010
But I'm not sure I want
to do it this way,

00:44:41.011 --> 00:44:42.185
I'm still thinking about it,

00:44:42.186 --> 00:44:43.770
but this is the way I think just

00:44:43.771 --> 00:44:45.430
in Stewart's talk today
is talking about you know

00:44:45.431 --> 00:44:47.360
adding this native thing to

00:44:47.361 --> 00:44:48.680
the other things and then it's

00:44:48.681 --> 00:44:50.270
going to pervade
everything, right?

00:44:50.271 --> 00:44:52.870
So, here too, we
have this kind of

00:44:52.871 --> 00:44:54.190
core musical thing
but then we have

00:44:54.191 --> 00:44:55.490
this extra information we want to

00:44:55.491 --> 00:44:57.225
add and how can we
deal with that.

00:44:57.226 --> 00:45:00.010
And so here there's
another ornaments

00:45:00.011 --> 00:45:03.835
which Talia nicely had already
talked about in her talk,

00:45:03.836 --> 00:45:05.240
which is kind of
you can think of as

00:45:05.241 --> 00:45:07.300
a generalization of
equivalences where now

00:45:07.301 --> 00:45:10.775
you have more information
on one side than the other.

00:45:10.776 --> 00:45:12.250
And again, you can kind of do

00:45:12.251 --> 00:45:14.695
this automatic lifting
and so forth with that.

00:45:14.696 --> 00:45:19.390
So, this is also something
I'm adding to this.

00:45:19.391 --> 00:45:21.760
Here the main challenge has
been that all this work

00:45:21.761 --> 00:45:24.400
although it's actually done
in AGDA which is nice for me,

00:45:24.401 --> 00:45:27.125
they did all with deep
embeddings which made it very

00:45:27.126 --> 00:45:30.570
nice for them to do
theoretical things with it,

00:45:30.571 --> 00:45:32.340
but we need a shallow
embedding in AGDA.

00:45:32.341 --> 00:45:35.190
So, I'm using
reflection mechanism in

00:45:35.191 --> 00:45:36.630
AGDA to try to convert between

00:45:36.631 --> 00:45:38.750
the deep and
the shallow embeddings.

00:45:38.751 --> 00:45:42.560
So, to conclude I
think this has been

00:45:42.561 --> 00:45:44.820
a great application area

00:45:44.821 --> 00:45:47.860
and I'm looking forward
to playing with it more.

00:45:48.920 --> 00:45:51.870
Just like types,
people don't want

00:45:51.871 --> 00:45:55.600
to put in the effort
to do math at first,

00:45:55.601 --> 00:45:58.340
it's a lot of work, but I
think it pays off in the end.

00:45:58.341 --> 00:46:00.170
And then part of

00:46:00.171 --> 00:46:01.750
the goal of this project
is to figure out how to

00:46:01.751 --> 00:46:03.360
minimize that work and

00:46:03.361 --> 00:46:06.090
still get the rewards
of using the math.

00:46:12.970 --> 00:46:16.310
&gt;&gt; Any questions for John?

00:46:16.311 --> 00:46:20.105
So, starting, Earl.
Oh go head. Yes.

00:46:20.106 --> 00:46:23.975
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] just
too early to sing.

00:46:23.976 --> 00:46:27.930
&gt;&gt; It's too early in a way,

00:46:27.931 --> 00:46:29.790
but I think already
the equivalences stuff

00:46:29.791 --> 00:46:31.260
has been really nice.

00:46:31.261 --> 00:46:37.045
So, it's very promising. Yeah.

00:46:37.046 --> 00:46:41.935
&gt;&gt; So, this is for on
the AI side on styled transfers,

00:46:41.936 --> 00:46:45.060
people do it are also presumably

00:46:45.061 --> 00:46:49.820
music you like and to
make a box style so,

00:46:49.821 --> 00:46:51.190
does that relate to this?

00:46:51.191 --> 00:46:52.750
&gt;&gt; Yeah, this is intended to be

00:46:52.751 --> 00:46:54.475
a foundation to do that stuff.

00:46:54.476 --> 00:46:56.090
There's actually
even a lot of other work,

00:46:56.091 --> 00:46:58.340
there's a lot of stuff
in Haskell going on now,

00:46:58.341 --> 00:47:00.340
there's something called
the harm trace to things to do

00:47:00.341 --> 00:47:02.810
harmonic analyses and they use

00:47:02.811 --> 00:47:04.605
the type system to enforce

00:47:04.606 --> 00:47:06.375
harmonic rules and
stuff like that.

00:47:06.376 --> 00:47:08.210
And this is, again,
this is intended to

00:47:08.211 --> 00:47:10.080
be a base system that
all that can be built

00:47:10.081 --> 00:47:11.510
out of and I think I can

00:47:11.511 --> 00:47:13.590
redo for example
the harm trace stuff using

00:47:13.591 --> 00:47:15.370
the peno types will
be a lot more elegant

00:47:15.371 --> 00:47:16.570
than what they're doing now with

00:47:16.571 --> 00:47:18.630
a lot of Haskell
extensions again.

00:47:18.631 --> 00:47:21.315
&gt;&gt; So, you mentioned
that you need

00:47:21.316 --> 00:47:22.430
a shallow embedding is that for

00:47:22.431 --> 00:47:24.145
the media interface
through Haskell?

00:47:24.146 --> 00:47:25.900
&gt;&gt; No. That's just to
get the ornaments stuff

00:47:25.901 --> 00:47:27.525
to work on real AGDA code.

00:47:27.526 --> 00:47:30.050
So right now you'd need
to, in the deep embedding,

00:47:30.051 --> 00:47:33.150
you need to define
all your types and as this

00:47:33.151 --> 00:47:34.850
embedded thing
instead of using like

00:47:34.851 --> 00:47:37.655
real natural numbers
or whatever even.

00:47:37.656 --> 00:47:40.790
So, they define these
things as fixed points of

00:47:40.791 --> 00:47:44.020
functors in their deep embedding
and things like that.

00:47:44.021 --> 00:47:45.620
So, it's a matter of translating

00:47:45.621 --> 00:47:47.660
between these two worlds.

00:47:49.730 --> 00:47:53.125
&gt;&gt; Okay, any more
questions for John?

00:47:53.126 --> 00:47:57.250
All right. So, let's
thank John again.

